      "id": 1,
      "text": "Munhall was incorporated on June 24, 1901, and named after John Munhall, the original owner of the town site.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munhall%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The borough lies eight miles south of the confluence of the Monongahela and Allegheny rivers, abutting the borough of Homestead.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munhall%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A large part of the historic Homestead Works of the Carnegie Steel Company extended into Munhall, shaping its industrial past.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munhall%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Carnegie Library of Homestead, donated by Andrew Carnegie in 1896, sits in Munhall and remains a cultural landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Munhall%2C_Pennsylvania"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Munhall’s waterfront shopping and entertainment complex sits beside historic steel sites, blending modern life with industrial heritage.",
